---
import { repoUrl } from '../config';
---

<section id="build" class="section build">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="badge badge-purple">Hack on it</span>
      <h2>Build from source</h2>
      <p>
        VibeStudio can be built in Android Studio, VS Code with Gradle, or straight from Termux on the phone
        itself using the standalone <code>build.sh</code>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <h3>1. Clone with submodules</h3>
        <p class="muted">The terminal core (<code>libtermux-android</code>) lives in a git submodule.</p>
        <pre><code>git clone --recursive {repoUrl}.git
cd {repoUrl.split('/').pop()}</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <h3>2. Install the toolchain (Termux)</h3>
        <p class="muted">One-time setup — installs JDK, Kotlin, aapt2, d8 and friends.</p>
        <pre><code>./install_dependencies_termux.sh</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <h3>3. Build the APK</h3>
        <p class="muted">Compiles, dexes, aligns and signs. Output lands in <code>bin/VibeStudio.apk</code>.</p>
        <pre><code>./build.sh</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="more muted">
      Prefer a desktop? Open the project in Android Studio or run <code>./gradlew :app:assembleDebug</code>. See the
      <a href={`${repoUrl}#building--development`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">README</a> for VS Code tasks, adb
      setup and debugging tips.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .build {
    background: var(--bg-elev);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .steps {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .step h3 {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .step p {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .more {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }
</style>
